{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "מי יאכילנו בשר", :translit "mi ya'achilenu basar", :gloss "Who will feed us meat"} {:he "אף על פי שהיה להם בשר הרבה", :translit "af al pi shehayah lahem basar harbeh", :gloss "even though there was to them meat much"} {:he "הם היו סבורין לעבור הירדן מהרה", :translit "hem hayu s'vorin la'avor hayarden m'hera", :gloss "they were disposed to cross the Jordan soon"} {:he "אנו צריכין להלחם בכנענים", :translit "anu tz'richim l'hilachem b'ch'na'anim", :gloss "we need to fight with Canaanites"} {:he "ולא נוכל להעביר מקננו עמנו", :translit "v'lo nuchal l'ha'aver mik'neinu imanu", :gloss "and we will not be able to bring our livestock with us"} {:he "מי יאכילנו בשר", :translit "mi ya'achilenu basar", :gloss "Who will feed us meat"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Reads the demand for meat as practical anticipation of wartime logistics, not simple greed."}], :frame {:text "Numbers 11:4 records the riffraff's craving and Israel's weeping 'Who will feed us meat.' The complaint follows the verse's own description of Israel's abundant flocks and herds (Numbers 11:22, where Moses notes the vast livestock among them). The Ba'al HaTurim supplies a tactical motive: they expected to cross the Jordan and fight the Canaanites imminently, as the conquest narrative in Joshua 3–6 describes, and feared their livestock could not be transported during the crossing and campaign. Deuteronomy 3:18–20 frames the crossing of the Jordan as the prelude to battle for the land.", :sources ["Numbers 11:4" "Numbers 11:22" "Deuteronomy 3:18" "Joshua 3:1"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Reads the demand for meat as practical anticipation of wartime logistics, not simple greed.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:28:17.651730507Z", :model "glm-5.2"}